        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Jeu de Carte Sipa',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Jeu de Carte Sipa (or simply Sipa) is a popular trick-taking game in Togo and across West Africa. It is a variant of the regional game 'Spar'. The game is fast-paced and revolves around the critical importance of the final trick. The ultimate goal is to win the last trick(s), specifically with a 7, which provides significantly higher points.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Derived from 'Spar', which has roots in Dutch or Portuguese card traditions brought to the West African coast. It has adapted into a unique social game played in Togo, particularly in rural villages during the dry season.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Usually 3 or 4 players.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '35 cards: A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6. **Note**: The Ace of Spades is usually removed, making the King of Spades the highest spade.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '35 cards: A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6. **Note**: The Ace of Spades is usually removed, making the King of Spades the highest spade.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dealer deals 5 cards to each player (in batches of 3 then 2). The winner of the previous hand deals next.',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
